What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/
Date:		October, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.3
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		One directory per instance of a CXL Hot-range Monitoring Unit
		(CHMU, CXL r4.0 section 8.2.8): block Y of memdev X, instance Z.
		An instance counts accesses to units of the device's memory and
		reports the units whose count crossed a threshold in a hotlist.
		It is driven by one consumer at a time.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/consumer
Date:		October, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.3
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RW) The consumer driving the instance: the name of a registered
		in-kernel consumer ("perf" for the perf PMU with
		CONFIG_CXL_HMU_PERF, "damon" to hand hot units to DAMOS with
		CONFIG_CXL_HMU_DAMON) or "none". The default is "perf" on an
		instance that has a PMU, else "none". Writing a name while the
		current consumer has the instance running fails with -EBUSY; a
		name that is not registered with -ENOENT; any consumer on an
		instance without an interrupt with -EOPNOTSUPP; anything while
		the instance is being removed with -ENODEV. The current consumer
		is detached before the new one is attached, so if the new one
		refuses (its error is returned) the instance is left on "none".


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/mode
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/unit_size
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/hotness_threshold
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/epoch_ms
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/access_type
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/downsampling_factor
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/randomized_downsampling
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_base
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_size
Date:		October, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.3
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RW) The configuration a kernel consumer programs when it starts
		the instance. While the consumer is "perf" these are read-only
		(-EPERM) and show what the last perf event programmed; a store
		while the instance is active fails with -EBUSY. Each value is
		checked against the instance's capabilities at store time: a
		value the instance does not support is refused with -EOPNOTSUPP,
		an out-of-range one with -EINVAL or -ERANGE, and a refused store
		leaves the old value.

		mode: "epoch" (a unit is reported when its count crosses the
		threshold within an epoch, and the count is reported with it)
		or "always_on" (a unit is reported once its count crosses the
		threshold; the reported count is not valid), one of
		supported_modes.

		unit_size: bytes per counted unit, a power of two from
		supported_unit_sizes. It is programmed into the (disabled)
		instance at once, so counter_width reflects it.

		hotness_threshold: the count at which a unit is reported;
		below 1 << counter_width, else -ERANGE.

		epoch_ms: the epoch length in milliseconds, clamped to
		epoch_range_ms and rounded to what the device encodes; reads
		back the effective value, which is 0 for an epoch shorter than
		a millisecond. 0 is -EINVAL; a length the device cannot encode
		is -ERANGE.

		access_type: which requests are counted: "nontee_r",
		"nontee_w", "nontee_rw", "r", "w" or "rw", one of
		supported_access_types.

		downsampling_factor: count one request in this many, a power of
		two from supported_downsampling.

		randomized_downsampling: 0 or 1, if the instance supports it.

		range_base, range_size: the device physical address range
		counted, in units of 256 MiB; base + size must not exceed
		capacity_ranges, size must not be 0 and the range must hold at
		least one unit. The default is the whole device.


